.TH TSFS 1 2026-10-13 "tsfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tsfs \- time-aware storage filesystem: FUSE mount with retention policies and a garbage collector
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY tsfs
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-fuse\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-stack\-trace | \-\-no\-stack\-trace ]
.RB [ \-\-core\-dump | \-\-no\-core\-dump ]
.YS
.SY tsfs
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR level ]
.B \-\-check\-config
|
.B \-\-show\-config
|
.B \-\-dry\-run
|
.B \-\-status
|
.B \-\-stats
.YS
.SY tsfs
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR path ]
.B \-\-purge
.RB [ \-\-force | \-\-dry\-run ]
.YS
.SY tsfs
.B \-\-print\-example\-config
|
.B \-V
|
.B \-h
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tsfs
mounts a directory through FUSE and deletes files once their retention period has
elapsed, according to the rules of the configuration file.
.PP
The
.I backing directory
.RB ( filesystem.backing_directory )
is exposed unchanged at the
.I mount point
.RB ( filesystem.mountpoint ).
Applications use the mount point as an ordinary filesystem.
A built-in garbage collector evaluates every regular file against the retention
rules: a file expires at its modification time (or, optionally, its creation time)
plus the retention of the rule that matches it, and is deleted once expired.
Exclusion rules protect files absolutely; files matched by no rule follow
.BR retention.default ,
which is
.B never
unless configured otherwise.
.PP
Before each deletion, the file is validated again under a path lock: it must still
be the same regular file, inside the backing directory, not open through the mount,
and still expired under the current policy.
Directories, symbolic links and special files are never deleted.
.PP
With
.B security.stay_on_filesystem
(the default), entries on another filesystem than the backing directory (mount
points or bind-mounted files below it) are not reachable through the mount: the
operation fails with
.BR EXDEV .
A bind mount of the same filesystem is only detected for intermediate path
components, and only with
.BR openat2 (2);
set
.B security.require_openat2
where this matters.
New entries get the mode requested by the application after its own umask: the
daemon resets its umask to 0 at startup.
.PP
Without an action option,
.B tsfs
mounts the filesystem and runs as a daemon (in the background unless
.B \-f
or
.B \-d
is given) until it is unmounted or receives
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT .
The configuration is reloaded without remounting on
.B SIGHUP
and when the configuration file changes.
.PP
With
.B filesystem.retention_xattrs
(the default), every regular file of the mount has four read-only virtual
extended attributes:
.B user.tsfs.state
.RB ( expires ", " expired ", " excluded " or " never ),
.B user.tsfs.expires
(expiry in ISO 8601 UTC, only when the file expires),
.B user.tsfs.rule
(identifier of the deciding rule, or
.BR default )
and
.B user.tsfs.created
(birth time of the backing file in ISO 8601 UTC, or its modification time when the
backing filesystem does not report it).
They are read by name, for example
.BR "getfattr \-n user.tsfs.state" " \fIFILE\fR,"
and are not returned by
.BR listxattr (2),
so that
.BR ls (1)
does not mark every file with a
.BR + .
Other attribute names fail with
.BR ENODATA ;
the attributes cannot be set or removed.
.BR tsfs\-ls (1)
displays them.
.PP
Paths in retention rules are relative to the TSFS root:
.I /tmp/record
designates
.IR <backing_directory>/tmp/record .
The configuration format is described in
.IR configuration.md ,
installed with the documentation.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Actions
At most one action may be given; the default action mounts and runs the filesystem.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Validate the configuration and exit (0 = valid, 5 = invalid).
.TP
.B \-\-show\-config
Print the effective configuration (defaults included) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Scan the backing directory once, list the files that would be deleted now (same
checks as the real GC, except files open through a running mount, unknown here),
delete nothing, exit.
No mount is needed, and a running daemon is not affected.
When more files expire than
.B garbage_collector.max_pending_tasks
can hold, the list is incomplete and the summary line says so:
.RI "dry run: INCOMPLETE: " N " expiring file(s) did not fit in garbage_collector.max_pending_tasks (" X ") ..."
Raise that setting to list them all.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the status file of the running daemon
.RB ( metrics.status_file ).
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Scan the backing directory once and print, per rule that decides at least one
file (in configuration order,
.B default
for
.BR retention.default ),
the number of regular files and their size, how many are expired now, expire
within 24 hours, are excluded, or are kept forever
.RB ( never ),
and the next expiry (UTC); then a
.B TOTAL
row and the counts of directories, symbolic links, other entries, unreadable
entries and mount boundaries.
Needs read access to the whole backing tree.
.TP
.B \-\-purge
Delete now every file that is already expired, ignoring
.B garbage_collector.max_deletions_per_second
and
.BR max_deletions_per_cycle ,
with the usual validation of each file; excluded and
.B never
files are never touched.
Complete passes are repeated while the scheduler saturates, so a backlog larger
than
.B max_pending_tasks
is purged too.
Works with
.BR "garbage_collector.enabled: false" .
When a daemon serves the mount point (found through
.BR metrics.status_file :
live pid, process named
.BR tsfs ,
same mount point), it is sent
.B SIGUSR2
and does the purge; the command waits for the result in the status file and prints
.RI "purge: " N " file(s) deleted (" SIZE "), " F " failed, " O " kept because open through the mount" .
When the mount point is not mounted, this process purges the backing directory
itself.
When it is mounted but its daemon cannot be found, the purge is refused (status 7):
files open through the mount would not be protected; send
.B SIGUSR2
to the daemon instead.
With
.BR "garbage_collector.dry_run: true" ,
it is refused (status 7).
On a terminal, it asks for confirmation
.RB ( [y/N] ).
Exits with status 1 if a deletion failed.
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.BR \-\-purge :
do not ask for confirmation.
Required when standard input is not a terminal (otherwise status 2).
Rejected with any other action and with
.B \-\-purge \-\-dry\-run
(status 2).
.PP
.B \-\-purge \-\-dry\-run
(or
.BR "\-\-dry\-run \-\-purge" )
previews the purge: it prints exactly the
.B \-\-dry\-run
listing.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-example\-config
Print the commented example configuration.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print this help.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Configuration file (default:
.IR /etc/tsfs/config.yml ).
It must be a regular file (a symbolic link is followed and its target is checked).
When the effective user is root, it must also be owned by root and not writable by
its group or by others (fix with
.BR "chmod go\-w" );
otherwise
.B tsfs
exits with status 5.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mountpoint " \fIpath\fR"
Override
.BR filesystem.mountpoint .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-backing\-dir " \fIpath\fR"
Override
.BR filesystem.backing_directory .
.PP
The
.B \-m
and
.B \-b
values are normalized and validated like the values of the file (absolute, not
.IR / ,
not containing each other, not containing the log directory, the crash directory
or the status file); an invalid value exits with status 5 and a message starting
with
.IR "command line:" .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-level " \fIlevel\fR"
Override
.B logging.level
.RB ( trace ", " debug ", " info ", " warning ", " error ", " critical ).
.SS Running (default action only)
These options only apply when mounting: combined with an action, they are rejected
with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Do not detach from the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Foreground, log level debug, log also to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-fuse\-debug
Print every FUSE request (libfuse
.BR \-d ,
implies
.BR \-\-foreground ).
.TP
.B \-\-stack\-trace
Enable diagnostics with text crash reports.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-stack\-trace
Disable text crash reports.
.TP
.B \-\-core\-dump
Enable diagnostics with native core dumps.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-core\-dump
Do not change the core-dump limits.
.PP
Command-line values override the file for the whole life of the process,
including after a reload (SIGHUP or file change).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration file without remounting
.RB ( "systemctl reload tsfs"
sends it).
The new file is validated first; if it is invalid, the reload is rejected
.RB ( config_reload_failed )
and the running configuration stays active.
Changes to
.BR filesystem.* ", " diagnostics.* " and " security.*
require a restart and are reported, not applied.
The file is also watched with inotify, so saving it triggers the same reload.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Write the status file immediately and log a
.B status_requested
summary.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Purge: delete every expired file now, without rate limit (see
.BR \-\-purge ).
Logged as
.BR gc_purge_requested ", " gc_purge_started " and " gc_purge_completed .
Can be sent directly, for example
.B "systemctl kill \-s USR2 tsfs@NAME"
or
.BR "kill \-USR2" " \fIPID\fR."
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop the garbage collector, write a final status file, unmount the filesystem and
exit with status 0.
Unmounting the mount point externally
.RB ( umount ", " "fusermount3 \-u" )
has the same effect.
.PP
These signals are blocked from the very start of the daemon: one received during
startup is handled as soon as the filesystem serves requests (a
.B SIGTERM
then unmounts cleanly and exits with status 0).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tsfs/config.yml
Default configuration file (the build option
.B TSFS_DEFAULT_CONFIG_PATH
may change it).
Created from the example at installation only when absent; the example sets
.BR "garbage_collector.dry_run: true" .
.TP
.I /etc/tsfs/NAME.yml
Configuration of the TSFS filesystem
.IR NAME ,
served by the systemd template unit
.BR tsfs@NAME.service
(one daemon, one mount point and one backing directory per file).
.TP
.IR /lib/systemd/system/tsfs.service ", " tsfs@.service
systemd units (installation prefix dependent, e.g.
.IR /usr/local/lib/systemd/system ):
the first serves
.IR /etc/tsfs/config.yml ,
the template one filesystem per
.IR /etc/tsfs/NAME.yml .
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/doc/tsfs/examples/config.example.yml
Commented example configuration (also printed by
.BR \-\-print\-example\-config ).
.TP
.I /var/lib/tsfs/data
Default backing directory: the managed files are stored here.
It must exist before mounting.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tsfs/crash
Default directory of the text crash reports
.RI ( tsfs\-crash\- <time> \- <pid> .txt ,
mode 0600), when
.B diagnostics.enabled
is true.
A report holds the signal, the raw backtrace, a backtrace symbolized at crash time
(function+offset and module, from the faulting frame) and the memory map; the same
symbolized backtrace is printed on stderr.
File and line numbers are resolved offline with
.B scripts/tsfs\-symbolize.sh
from the source tree, with the same build.
.TP
.I /mnt/tsfs
Default mount point.
It must exist before mounting.
.TP
.I /var/log/tsfs/tsfs.log
Default log file, rotated to
.IR tsfs.log.1 " ... " tsfs.log.N
(optionally
.IR .gz ).
.TP
.I /run/tsfs/status.json
Default status file
.RB ( metrics.status_file ),
a JSON document rewritten periodically, on
.BR SIGUSR1 ,
when a purge starts or ends, and at shutdown.
.B \-\-purge
reads it to find the daemon.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/fuse/connections/ID
Kernel control directory of the FUSE connection of the mount (fusectl;
.I ID
is the device number of the mount,
.BR "stat \-c %d" " \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR)."
Its
.I waiting
file (requests not answered yet by the daemon) is copied into the
.B fuse
section of the status file;
.I abort
fails every pending request as a last resort.
.TP
.IR /usr/bin/ls ", " /usr/bin/ls.orig
By default, a root installation renames the system
.B ls
to
.I ls.orig
and installs the wrapper
.BR tsfs\-ls (1)
as
.IR /usr/bin/ls ;
the
.B uninstall
target renames
.I ls.orig
back.
.TP
.I tsfs.service
systemd unit (installed disabled) that runs
.B "tsfs \-\-config /etc/tsfs/config.yml \-\-foreground"
and reloads with
.BR SIGHUP .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error;
.BR \-\-purge :
at least one deletion failed, or the daemon stopped during the purge.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown option, incompatible options,
.B \-\-purge
without
.B \-\-force
when standard input is not a terminal).
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient privileges:
.I /dev/fuse
is not accessible, or
.B filesystem.allow_other
is set for a non-root daemon without
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ;
or
.B \-\-purge
cannot signal the daemon.
.TP
.B 4
Missing dependency (for example
.I /dev/fuse
does not exist, or
.B security.require_openat2
on a kernel without
.BR openat2 (2)).
.TP
.B 5
Invalid configuration (including a missing configuration file, a configuration
file a root daemon does not trust, and invalid
.BR \-m / \-b
values).
.TP
.B 7
Unexpected system state (missing backing directory or mount point, unwritable log
file, mount failure, no status file for
.BR \-\-status ,
purge refused because
.B garbage_collector.dry_run
is true or because the mount point is mounted without a reachable daemon).
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate a configuration and print it with every default:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
tsfs \-\-check\-config \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml
tsfs \-\-show\-config \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml
.EE
.in
.PP
List the files the policy would delete now, without deleting anything:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sudo tsfs \-\-dry\-run \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml
.EE
.in
.PP
Count the files per rule, then delete every expired file now:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sudo tsfs \-\-stats \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml
sudo tsfs \-\-purge \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml          # asks [y/N]
sudo tsfs \-\-purge \-\-force \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml  # scripts
.EE
.in
.PP
Read the retention of a file through the mount:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
getfattr \-n user.tsfs.expires /mnt/tsfs/tmp/record/a.mp4
.EE
.in
.PP
A rule that deletes temporary recordings one day after their last modification,
with an exception for one directory:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
version: 1
retention:
  default: "never"
  rules:
    \- id: temporary\-recordings
      path: "/tmp/record"
      recursive: true
      retention: "1d"
    \- id: keep\-important\-recordings
      action: "exclude"
      path: "/tmp/record/keep"
      recursive: true
.EE
.in
.PP
Mount in the foreground with debug logging on stderr:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sudo tsfs \-c /etc/tsfs/config.yml \-d
.EE
.in
.PP
Run as a service, reload after editing the configuration, read the status:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now tsfs.service
sudo systemctl reload tsfs
sudo tsfs \-\-status
.EE
.in
.PP
Unmount:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sudo systemctl stop tsfs        # or: sudo umount /mnt/tsfs
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tsfs\-ls (1),
.BR getfattr (1),
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR coredumpctl (1),
.BR inotify (7),
.BR openat2 (2),
.BR statx (2).
.PP
The user guide
.RI ( user\-guide.md ),
the configuration reference
.RI ( configuration.md )
and the monitoring guide
.RI ( monitoring.md ),
installed in the TSFS documentation directory (by default
.IR /usr/local/share/doc/tsfs/user/ ).
